<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shota Rustaveli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 12th–early 13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shota Rustaveli was a Georgian poet of the late 12th and early 13th centuries, author of the epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-knight-in-the-panthers-skin">The Knight in the Panther's Skin</a>. He is thought to have served at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamar-of-georgia">Queen Tamar</a>, possibly as treasurer, and tradition says he ended his life at the Georgian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monastery-of-the-cross">Monastery of the Cross</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, where a fresco portrait of him survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shota-rustaveli.jpg" alt="Shota Rustaveli" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DerFuchs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shota_Rustaveli_fresco_in_the_Monastery_of_the_Cross_in_Jerusalem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Almost nothing is known about Rustaveli from contemporary sources. In the prologue of his poem the poet calls himself a certain Rustveli. This is not a surname but a territorial epithet, meaning of or holder of Rustavi, though a 10th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> fragment found in 1975 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-catherines-monastery">Saint Catherine's Monastery</a> in Sinai attests its use as a cognomen by a noble house of Ru(i)staveli. Georgian authors of the 15th to 18th centuries are more informative and almost unanimously name him Shota Rustaveli.</p><p>Both folk tradition and the 17th-century royal poet Archil II make him a native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Meskheti</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, where his home village of Rustavi lay. A legend says he was educated at the academies of Gelati and Ikalto and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, meaning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. He knew Persian well enough to read its poetry without translations and may have composed Persian verse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Rustaveli wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-knight-in-the-panthers-skin">The Knight in the Panther's Skin</a>, regarded as the national epic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. He must have composed it no earlier than the 1180s and no later than the first decade of the 13th century, most probably in 1205 to 1207. The poem is set in a fictional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, and events in those distant lands are read as an allegory of the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamar-of-georgia">Queen Tamar</a> and the size and glory of the Kingdom of Georgia in its Golden Age.</p><p>The poem was first printed in 1712 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>. Two folios from the 16th century and some lines from the 14th century are kept at the Georgian National Center of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscripts</a> in Tbilisi. All other known copies date from the 17th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A name preserved on a fresco and in a document from the former Georgian Monastery of the Holy Cross at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> identifies Shota as a sponsor of the monastery and a high treasurer, which echoes a popular legend that he served at Tamar's court and retired to the monastery in old age. The fresco, the only known contemporary portrait, was described by the Georgian pilgrim Timote Gabashvili in 1757 and rediscovered by Georgian scholars in 1960. It was defaced in 2004 and has since been restored.</p><p>The highest Georgian state prize for art and literature bears his name, as do Rustaveli Avenue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> and many other landmarks. The Hungarian painter Mihály Zichy made the illustrations most often used in editions of the poem.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shota Rustaveli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was the period of <b>Shota Rustaveli</b>’s epic The Knight in the Panther’s Skin, written around 1200 and dedicated to Tamar, which became the national poem of Georgia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The most famous work of Middle Georgian is The Knight in the Panther’s Skin, an epic poem written by <b>Shota Rustaveli</b> in the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/language/georgian">Georgian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rustavi, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. Died in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.62,43.13&amp;year=1264" aria-label="Rustavi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Shota Rustaveli"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5832 3965h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5832 3965h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.62,43.13&amp;year=1264">What was here in 1264? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
